---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/coderturtle/object-lesson";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const recentEntries = (await getCollection("buildlog", ({ data }) => !data.draft))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf())
  .slice(0, 3);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Object Lesson: guide"
  description="Learn to think in objects the way you already work, harness in hand, and let the compiler and a real test suite, not a quiz, be the first gate."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Object Lesson</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 text-lg opacity-80">
    Learn to think in objects the way you already work: harness in hand, letting the compiler and
    a real test suite, not a quiz, be the first gate.
  </p>

  <div class="post-body mt-10">
    <p>
      This page is orientation for anyone landing on the deployed site rather than the repo
      directly. Object Lesson teaches object-oriented programming in Java to first-year students
      who've never written a class before, but who already drive a coding agent (a
      <strong>harness</strong>: Claude Code, Codex, or an equivalent tool) every day. The nine
      modules themselves, plus the reasoning behind their order, live in the
      <a href={REPO}>repo</a>, whose own README covers the same ground in more detail once you've
      cloned it. Nothing here substitutes for cloning it; this is orientation, not the content.
    </p>

    <h2>Two gates, not one</h2>
    <p>
      Every exercise runs through your own harness. A <strong>deterministic gate</strong> checks
      first, a mechanical pass/fail with no judgment involved: <code>javac</code> compiles clean,
      JUnit tests are green, Checkstyle is clean. Then a <strong>conceptual gate</strong>:
      Coachgremlin, this workshop's teaching agent (a role you run yourself, inside your own
      harness, not a hosted service), checks whether the design underneath is real, whether an
      encapsulation actually protects an invariant or is just a getter and setter pasted onto
      every field, whether inheritance was the right call or just the first one that compiled. A
      class that compiles and passes its tests has cleared the first gate only; that is necessary,
      never sufficient proof the design is sound.
    </p>

    <h2>New to the subject, not new to building software</h2>
    <p>
      This workshop assumes basic programming literacy: variables, conditionals, loops, functions,
      in any language. What it does not assume is any prior object-oriented programming or Java
      syntax, both are treated as genuinely new. That is a different gap than a practitioner
      picking up an unfamiliar language: here, the subject itself, not just its syntax, is the
      thing being learned for the first time.
    </p>

    <h2>The arc</h2>
    <p>
      Nine modules, each stating its real prerequisite rather than assuming you read the last one:
      Classes & Objects, Constructors, Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, Abstraction (with
      a required-but-light Generics & Collections baseline, plus an optional deeper extension),
      Composition vs. Inheritance, Exception Handling, then a synthesis capstone that hands you a
      broken design and asks which concept is actually the root cause. Full reasoning for the
      order, and the curriculum research it's checked against, is in the repo's
      <code>docs/workshop-design.md</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>What you walk away with</h2>
    <p>
      Two different things get built during each module: your own judgment, and a reusable
      artifact your agent can load next time. See each module's own README (once authored) for the
      evidence behind its specific gate.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>You</strong> get practiced judgment: reading a compiler error and finding the real
        fix instead of a workaround, telling a real invariant apart from a cosmetic accessor, and
        deciding whether a relationship between two classes is genuinely "is-a" or actually
        "has-a."
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your agent</strong> gets the packaged <strong>takeaways</strong>, artifacts you
        keep past the exercise itself: a class-vs-object scaffolding checklist, a constructor
        initialization checklist, an invariant-audit checklist, an is-a litmus test, a
        dynamic-dispatch debugging playbook, an interface-vs-abstract-class decision guide, a
        composition-vs-inheritance decision guide, a custom-exception-hierarchy template, and a
        personal OOP diagnostic playbook tying it all together.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The runbook</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Prerequisites: basic programming literacy in any language (variables, conditionals, loops,
        functions); already using a coding-agent harness regularly; a JDK installed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Clone it: <code>git clone {REPO}.git</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        Start at <code>modules/README.md</code>. It has the full arc, in order, and a table of
        what each module's gate requires and what you keep from it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Work through the nine modules in order. Each states its real prerequisite, and, where this
        workshop chose an order beyond what's structurally required, says so rather than dressing
        a preference up as a rule.
      </li>
      <li>
        No module completes by reading it. Every module states a required gate, checked first
        mechanically, then conceptually. If a module ever reduces to "read this, then move on," or
        a link here is broken, that's a defect: <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>open an issue</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  {
    recentEntries.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2 class="mt-14 text-xl font-semibold">Recent from the build log</h2>
        <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          {recentEntries.map((entry) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`${base}build-log/${entry.id}/`} class="entry-card">
                <p class="entry-title text-lg font-semibold">{entry.data.title}</p>
                <p class="mt-1 text-sm opacity-80">{entry.data.description}</p>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-6 text-sm opacity-70">
          See the <a href={`${base}build-log/`} class="underline">full index</a>.
        </p>
      </>
    )
  }
</BaseLayout>
